P1B1/1B3-mediated uptake to optimize efficacy and minimize adverse events [Data: Relationships (26321, 26323, 26332, 26336, 26340)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Non-statin drugs also exhibit clinically relevant OATP1B1/1B3 interactions\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond statins, several non-statin therapeutics interact with OATP1B1 or OATP1B3.柳氮磺胺嘧啶 interacts with BCRP (a related transporter), while阿曲生坦, E3S,多烯紫杉醇, and胆酰牛磺酸 demonstrate direct interactions with OATP1B1 or OATP1B3. Notably,多烯紫杉醇’s pharmacokinetics are influenced by an OATP1B3 intronic variant (IVS12-5676A>G), which increases drug exposure (AUC) and reduces clearance. These findings illustrate that OATP-mediated transport affects a wide therapeutic spectrum, including anticancer agents, antihypertensives, and endogenous steroids [Data: Entities (5968, 8663, 9108, 2861, 4820, 8060, 8108); Relationships (39811, 4427, 26321, 26332, 26340, 26347, 26348)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Drug classes beyond statins are modulated by OATP1B1 activity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"OATP1B1 interactions extend to multiple drug classes, including列奈类 (e.g., nateglinide) and血管紧张肽转换酶抑制剂 (ACE inhibitors). These associations suggest that transporter inhibition or genetic variation could alter the pharmacokinetics of glucose-lowering and antihypertensive medications. Although specific clinical outcomes for these classes are less detailed in the provided data compared to statins, their inclusion in transporter interaction networks implies potential for clinically significant drug–drug or gene–drug interactions requiring monitoring [Data: Relationships (26324, 26330)].\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 7.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community poses a high clinical impact due to the central role of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 in determining the safety and efficacy of widely used medications, particularly statins, with strong evidence linking genetic variants t